(no 1 ) : Lab Introduction

This lab demonstrates a Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) bypass vulnerability in a web application. Two-factor authentication 
is designed to add an additional security layer during login by requiring users to provide a second verification factor,
usually a code sent through email, SMS, or an authenticator app. The goal of this lab is to access the victim user's account 
page without possessing the required 2FA verification code. Even though the attacker already has valid login credentials, the 
application should not allow account access without completing the second authentication step. However, due to improper access 
control logic, the application allows users to bypass the 2FA verification stage and directly access the protected account
page.

(no 2 ) : What Is in This Lab

In this lab, the attacker already knows both the username and password of the victim account. Normally, after entering
correct credentials, the application should block further access until the user successfully enters the 2FA verification code.
The application sends the verification code to the user's email, which acts as the second authentication factor. However, the
web application fails to properly enforce this verification step on the backend. Because of this flaw, an attacker can skip 
the verification process by directly navigating to a protected URL such as the account page.

(no 3 ) : What Is the Vulnerability in This Lab

The vulnerability present in this lab is called a 2FA bypass due to improper authentication state validation. The web 
application does not correctly verify whether the user has completed the second authentication step before granting access
to sensitive pages. Instead of validating the 2FA status on the server side, the application only relies on the client-side
navigation flow. This means that if a user manually changes the URL to the account page after submitting valid login 
credentials, the server still allows access even though the 2FA process has not been completed.

(no 4 ) : How to Solve the Lab

To solve the lab, first log in using the provided credentials for the normal user account. After logging in, the 
application will ask for a 2FA verification code sent via email. The lab provides an email client to view the code. Once
logged in successfully, navigate to the account page and note the URL of that page. Then log out of the account and log in
again, this time using the victim’s credentials. When the application asks for the verification code, instead of entering
the code, manually modify the URL in the browser to access the account page directly (for example /my-account). Because the
server does not validate the 2FA state properly, the page loads successfully and the lab is solved.

(no 5 ) : Why This Vulnerability Exists and Why It Is Dangerous

This vulnerability exists because the application incorrectly assumes that users will follow the intended authentication 
flow. Instead of verifying on the server whether the 2FA step has been completed, the application relies on the frontend
navigation process. This is dangerous because attackers can easily manipulate URLs or requests. If an attacker already has 
stolen or leaked credentials, bypassing 2FA removes the final security barrier protecting user accounts. In real-world 
applications, this could allow attackers to gain full access to user data, financial information, personal messages, or 
administrative functions.

(no 6 ) : How Dangerous This Vulnerability Is

A 2FA bypass vulnerability is considered high severity because two-factor authentication is specifically designed to 
protect accounts even when passwords are compromised. If this mechanism fails, attackers can gain access to accounts with 
only the username and password. In applications such as banking platforms, cloud services, or email systems, this 
vulnerability could lead to account takeover, financial theft, data breaches, and identity fraud.

(no 7 ) : What Happens If This Vulnerability Is Not Fixed

If this vulnerability is not fixed, attackers who obtain user credentials through phishing, credential stuffing, or data 
leaks can easily bypass the additional security layer. This makes the entire two-factor authentication system ineffective. 
Organizations relying on this system would falsely assume their users are protected while attackers could still gain 
unauthorized access to sensitive accounts.

(no 8 ) : Other Ways to Exploit This Vulnerability

Attackers may exploit this vulnerability in multiple ways. Instead of manually changing the URL in a browser, they can
intercept requests using tools such as proxy tools and directly send requests to protected endpoints like the account page.
Automated scripts could also be used to test whether protected pages can be accessed without completing the 2FA process. 
If the application fails to verify authentication state properly, attackers can systematically bypass the verification
stage and gain access to restricted resources.

(no 9 ) : Using Python to Test or Exploit This Vulnerability

Python can be used to automate the exploitation process by sending HTTP requests directly to the web application. After
submitting valid login credentials, the script can attempt to access the protected account endpoint without completing
the 2FA verification step. If the server allows access, the script confirms the presence of the vulnerability. Libraries
such as HTTP request libraries can be used to maintain sessions and send authenticated requests automatically. This allows
security researchers to test whether applications enforce the 2FA verification step correctly.

(no 10 ) : Can a Normal Person Exploit This Vulnerability

Yes, even a person with limited technical knowledge can exploit this vulnerability if they understand basic web navigation 
and URL manipulation. Since the attack only requires changing the URL or sending a direct request to a protected page, 
advanced hacking skills are not necessary. This makes the vulnerability even more dangerous because attackers do not 
require sophisticated tools or deep programming knowledge to exploit it.

(no 11 ) : over all view : conclusion : 

This lab demonstrates how improper authentication logic can completely break the purpose of two-factor authentication.
Even though 2FA is intended to provide stronger security, failing to validate the authentication state on the server 
side can allow attackers to bypass the entire process. Developers must ensure that every protected endpoint verifies
whether the user has successfully completed all authentication steps before granting access.

